# Gitleaks false positives, ignored by exact fingerprint.
#
# The generic-api-key rule matches `appAccountToken` / `expectedAppAccountToken` in the test
# fixtures because the field name ends in "Token". They are UUIDs standing in for the value an iOS
# client passes to Product.purchase(options:) — not credentials, and not secret.
#
# Fingerprints rather than a rule allowlist, deliberately. A rule allowlist scoped to `test/` would
# suppress every generic-api-key finding across the whole test tree; a fingerprint pins one finding
# in one file at one line, so a secret that is new, moved, or of another kind still reports.
#
# Verified: with these in place, a realistic GitHub PAT planted in `src/config.ts` and in
# `test/unit/storekit-router.test.ts` is reported in both cases.
#
# Both scan modes are listed: `gitleaks detect` reads git history, `--no-git` reads the working
# tree, and the two fingerprint differently.
